"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/pubs.aip.org\/jcp\/article\/57\/9\/3587\/769194\/Shock-Tube-Calorimeter-for-the-Dissociation-Energy"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[1972,11,1]]},"references-count":13,"journal-issue":{"issue":"9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[1972,11,1]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/1.1678811","ISSN":["0021-9606","1089-7690"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0021-9606","type":"print"},{"value":"1089-7690","type":"electronic"}],"published-other":{"date-parts":[[1972,11,1]]},"published":{"date-parts":[[1972,11,1]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,28]],"date-time":"2025-10-28T03:07:29Z","timestamp":1761620849479},"reference-count":5,"publisher":"AIP Publishing","issue":"8","content-domain":{"domain":["pubs.aip.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[1972,10,15]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The time-dependent Nernst-Planck equations are used to derive analytical expressions for the ionic concentrations and electric potential as a function of position and time as equilibrium is approached. For sufficiently large values of time it is shown that the concentrations of cations and anions approach equilibrium with the same exponential decay constant, for which an explicit formula is derived. It is also shown that even for large times deviations from electroneutrality occur. A comparison of the analytical solutions is made with the numerical solutions, which are calculated by solving the time-dependent Nernst-Planck equations on a digital computer. Excellent agreement is obtained.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/1.1678749","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2004,2,6]],"date-time":"2004-02-06T21:02:35Z","timestamp":1076101355000},"page":"3263-3265","update-policy":"http:\/\/dx.doi.org\/10.1063\/aip-crossmark-policy-page","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":7,"title":["Solutions of the Nernst-Planck Equations for Ionic Diffusion for Conditions near Equilibrium"],"prefix":"10.1063","volume":"57","author":[{"given